diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index 9fa227f7..637b2dab 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -2,7 +2,69 @@
 
 ## Unreleased
 
-* None yet.
+* Fix: a Kotlin `value class` that is not visible from Moshi's own package can now be decoded by the
+  reflective adapter. Reading one raised `java.lang.IllegalAccessException`, which `fromJson` does not
+  declare, because the `box-impl` method it is constructed through was never made accessible the way
+  the constructor already was. Writing was unaffected, and `public` and `internal` value classes were
+  unaffected on both sides.
+* Fix: `Moshi.adapter(KType)`, and the reified `Moshi.adapter<T>()` built on it, now apply the type's
+  nullability marking for every type instead of only for those whose adapter is not already
+  null-safe. `moshi.adapter<String>()` accepted a `null` document and returned `null` into a
+  non-nullable Kotlin `String`; the same was true of enums, collections, maps, arrays and `Any`,
+  while `Int`, `Long`, `Double` and `Boolean` already behaved correctly. **This changes behaviour for
+  existing callers**: a non-nullable type argument now refuses a `null` document with
+  `JsonDataException`, which is what the method's documentation has always promised. Ask for the
+  nullable type - `moshi.adapter<String?>()` - to accept one.
+* Fix: an `IOException` raised by a `@ToJson` or `@FromJson` method now reaches the caller as a
+  `RuntimeException` carrying it as the cause, instead of as `java.lang.AssertionError`, from
+  `JsonAdapter.toJson(T)`, `toJsonValue(T)` and `fromJsonValue(Object)`. Those three cannot declare
+  `IOException` and each assumed the only possible source was its own Buffer or object graph, which
+  never fail; the adapter stack above them can, and the documented `@ToJson`/`@FromJson` signatures
+  say so. The entry points that do declare `IOException` - `fromJson(String)`, `fromJson(JsonReader)`
+  and `toJson(BufferedSink, T)` - are unchanged and still deliver it untouched.
+* Fix: an exception thrown by a Java `record`'s canonical constructor or by one of its component
+  accessors now reaches the caller unchanged instead of as `java.lang.AssertionError`. A record that
+  validates in a compact constructor is the ordinary way to reject bad input, and its
+  `IllegalArgumentException` was being rewritten into an `Error` no caller catches. The adapter
+  guarded its `MethodHandle` invocations with a `catch (InvocationTargetException)`, but a
+  `MethodHandle` propagates its target's throwable directly rather than wrapping it, so that branch
+  never ran. Reporting an absent primitive component as `JsonDataException` is unchanged.
+* Fix: the built-in `char`/`Character` adapter now throws `JsonDataException` for an empty JSON
+  string instead of `StringIndexOutOfBoundsException`.
+* Fix: `JsonAdapter.fromJsonValue()` now refuses a number it cannot represent exactly, matching what
+  `fromJson()` already did for the equivalent literal. Reading `1.5`, `1e19`, `NaN` or `Infinity` as
+  a `long`, or `1.5`, `3e9` or `Long.MAX_VALUE` as an `int`, previously returned a truncated,
+  saturated or wrapped value and now throws `JsonDataException`. A numeric string that cannot be
+  represented exactly no longer escapes as `java.lang.ArithmeticException` either.
+* Fix: `JsonAdapter.fromJsonValue()` now throws `JsonDataException` for a non-finite `Double` or
+  `Float`, such as `NaN` or an infinity, instead of `java.lang.AssertionError`. The reader behind
+  it raised `JsonEncodingException`, which is an `IOException`, and `fromJsonValue()` converts an
+  `IOException` into an `AssertionError`. Reading bytes is unchanged: a document whose text says
+  `NaN` is still a `JsonEncodingException`.
+* Fix: the Kotlin `subtypeOf<T>()` and `supertypeOf<T>()` helpers now work for a primitive-backed
+  type argument. `subtypeOf<Int>()` failed with `IllegalArgumentException: Unexpected primitive int.
+  Use the boxed type.` because the table that boxes primitives mapped each one to itself.
+* Fix: every read on a closed `JsonAdapter.fromJsonValue()` reader now throws
+  `IllegalStateException: JsonReader is closed`, matching the reader over JSON text. `hasNext()`
+  returned true, `skipValue()` returned normally having done nothing, and `nextInt()`, `nextLong()`
+  and `nextDouble()` threw a `JsonDataException` naming the reader's internal closed marker.
+* Fix: `EnumJsonAdapter` now reports the JSON path of the value it refused. It read the path after
+  consuming the value, so the message named the following array element - a bad value at `$[0]` was
+  reported `at path $[1]`. Object paths were already correct.
+* Fix: `Rfc3339DateJsonAdapter` now refuses a date that does not exist when the value carries no
+  time component. `"2015-02-30"`, `"2015-09-31"`, `"2015-13-26"` and `"2015-99-99"` were rolled
+  forward into a different date - `"2015-99-99"` decoded to 2023-06-07 - because that path used a
+  lenient calendar while the path for a full date-time did not. Values with a time component are
+  unaffected, and a date with no time is still interpreted in the host time zone as before.
+* Docs: `JsonDataException` now documents the nesting limit it is actually raised at. Its KDoc said
+  the exception may be triggered past 31 levels; readers and writers have accepted 255 levels since
+  the limit was raised from 32, so the documented bound was eight times tighter than the real one.
+  No behavior changed.
+* Docs: `JsonReader`'s `@throws` clauses now name the exceptions its readers actually raise.
+  `nextString()`, `nextBoolean()` and `nextNull()` throw `IllegalStateException` when the reader is
+  closed, not `JsonDataException`; `nextDouble()` reports a non-finite value as
+  `JsonEncodingException` when reading JSON text and as `JsonDataException` when reading an object
+  graph. No behavior changed.
 
 
